
; ======================================================================
;  hw_addr_q.pi4 - the Arduino UNO Q's answer to "is this address safe
;  to touch".
;
;  The core asks HwAddrCheck() before every command that takes an address
;  from the operator; the contract is in Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi and the asking
;  side is Anvil/Core/addrsafe.pbi. This file is the table, and it is the
;  reason the seam exists at all.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHAT THIS SOC DOES, AND WHY IT IS WORSE THAN A FAULT
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  On the QCM2290 an MMIO access to a block whose clock is gated does not
;  fault, does not time out, and does not read back a bus-error pattern.
;  THE ACCESS NEVER COMPLETES. The processor stops on it, the console
;  stops mid-line, the network stops answering, and the board has to have
;  its power physically removed. No watchdog helps - the watchdog would
;  have to be serviced by a processor that is stalled - and no reset
;  command can be typed, because there is nothing left to type at.
;
;  It has happened three times on this board in two days, and the first
;  one is why this file exists. The whole event was one command:
;
;      memory 4AC0004 10
;
;  a plain read of the QUPv3 wrapper's own version register, typed at
;  Anvil's prompt as the first line of a scripted session. The board's
;  transcript is flushed after every command, so the log settles it
;  exactly: the line before completed, and that line has no echo in the
;  file at all.
;
;  THE I2C DRIVER'S OWN PRESENCE CHECK WAS CORRECT AND NEVER RAN.
;  `memory` goes straight to the address - that is what it is for - so a
;  guard living inside a driver protected nothing. That is the finding
;  this table is the answer to.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHERE EVERY ROW COMES FROM, AND WHY THE PROVENANCE IS IN THE TABLE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Three grades of evidence, and they are NOT merged, because merging
;  them is how "the device tree implies it is fine" turns into "the board
;  says it is safe" and then into somebody's afternoon:
;
;    PROVEN     read or written on THIS board, at THIS exception level,
;               under THIS firmware, and it answered (or it hung).
;               Only these earn SAFE.
;    MEASURED   read from Linux on this board over SSH, or observed by a
;               probe that got far enough to report. Strong, but Linux
;               runs after its own drivers have voted for clocks that a
;               UEFI application has not.
;    DECLARED   what the device tree and the vendor clock driver say.
;               Good enough to say "this is behind a GDSC, do not touch
;               it" - a positive claim of DANGER is safe to make from a
;               weaker source. NOT good enough to say SAFE.
;
;  THE ASYMMETRY IS DELIBERATE AND IT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN. A wrong
;  UNCLOCKED costs one refusal the operator can override in two words. A
;  wrong SAFE costs a power cycle and whatever was on the board. So
;  UNCLOCKED is claimed from DECLARED evidence and SAFE is claimed only
;  from PROVEN evidence, and everything else answers UNKNOWN and says it
;  is ignorance.
;
;  Sources, all in this repository:
;    ArduinoQ/Reference/agatti.dtsi                    the SoC device tree
;    ArduinoQ/Reference/unoq-live-device-tree.dts      read off THIS board
;    ArduinoQ/Reference/Adreno702/linux/drivers/clk/qcom/gcc-qcm2290.c
;    ArduinoQ/Reference/Adreno702/linux/drivers/clk/qcom/gpucc-qcm2290.c
;
;  A TRAP WORTH NAMING BEFORE THE TABLE: the six serial engines are BELOW
;  the wrapper, not inside it. SE0..SE5 run $04A80000..$04A97FFF and the
;  wrapper is at $04AC0000 (agatti.dtsi:1178-1180, :1190-1557). A range
;  check written as "the wrapper and its size" covers not one engine.
;
;  A SECOND ONE: the device tree's GMU window ($0596A000 + $30000) SWALLOWS
;  the whole of GPUCC ($05990000 + $9000). That overlap is real in the
;  source and it is an artefact of a shim - there is no true GMU on this
;  part (agatti.dtsi:1798, "qcom,adreno-gmu-wrapper"). The rows below
;  split it around GPUCC rather than reproducing the overlap, because
;  GPUCC is the one window in that whole region that has been read here.
; ======================================================================

; The block the last check matched, and the sentence naming it. Valid
; only immediately after a HwAddrCheck() that did not answer SAFE - the
; same rule, and the same reason, as memrange.pi4's gMonHitLo/gMonHitHi.
Global gQAddrLo.i
Global gQAddrHi.i
Global *gQAddrWhy

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  QAddrHit - does lo..hi touch blo..bhi AT ALL, and record it if so.
;
;  ANY OVERLAP COUNTS, and that is the right test for a dangerous block:
;  a 64-byte dump that starts four bytes before the aperture still reads
;  into it, and the read that stalls is the one inside. The monitor-
;  overlap check next door uses exactly this shape for exactly this
;  reason.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i QAddrHit(lo.i, hi.i, blo.i, bhi.i)
  If hi >= blo And lo <= bhi
    gQAddrLo = blo
    gQAddrHi = bhi
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  QAddrIn - is lo..hi WHOLLY INSIDE blo..bhi.
;
;  THE SAFE LIST USES THIS ONE AND NOT QAddrHit, and the difference is
;  load-bearing. "This range touches something known good" is not a
;  reason to allow it: the other end may be in something known bad. A
;  range earns SAFE only when every byte of it is inside one window this
;  board has actually read.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i QAddrIn(lo.i, hi.i, blo.i, bhi.i)
  If lo >= blo And hi <= bhi
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwAddrCheck(lo, hi, forWrite) - the answer.
;
;  ORDER MATTERS AND IT IS: dangerous first, then safe, then named
;  ignorance, then plain ignorance. Anything that touches a block known
;  not to answer is refused whatever else it also touches.
;
;  forWrite IS ACCEPTED AND NOT USED HERE, and that is honest rather than
;  lazy: on this part a READ of a gated block hangs exactly as hard as a
;  write. There is no block on this board that may be read but not
;  written, so there is nothing for the flag to say. It stays in the
;  signature because the seam is shared and a board that DOES have such a
;  block needs somewhere to say so.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwAddrCheck(lo.i, hi.i, forWrite.i)
  *gQAddrWhy = 0
  gQAddrLo = 0
  gQAddrHi = 0

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  1. KNOWN NOT TO ANSWER. Any overlap refuses the whole range.
  ; ==================================================================

  ; ---- THE QUPv3 SERIAL BLOCK. PROVEN, the hard way. ---------------
  ; The wrapper first, because it is the one that was actually typed.
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $04AC0000, $04AC1FFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the QUPv3 wrapper, the serial block's own control aperture: reading its version register at 04AC0004 is the exact command that stalled this board, and it took a power cycle to get it back"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  ; SE4 is carved out of the engine span BEFORE the span is tested, so
  ; that it can answer with its own sentence rather than the general one.
  ; It is the console engine and it is the one honest disagreement in
  ; this file - see the UNKNOWN section below, which is where it lands.
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $04A80000, $04A8FFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "one of QUP serial engines 0 to 3: two AHB clocks gate all six engines and the wrapper that owns them is proven dark here, so no engine in this aperture can answer"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $04A94000, $04A97FFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "QUP serial engine 5, the SPI link to the on-board microcontroller: the same two AHB clocks gate it and the wrapper is proven dark here"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  ; The GPI DMA engine sits in the same block's address neighbourhood and
  ; behind the same kind of vote (agatti.dtsi:1160, disabled by default).
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $04A00000, $04A5FFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the GPI DMA engine that feeds the serial block, which is voted for the same way the serial engines are"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf

  ; ---- THE GPU. The suspect in the third hang. ---------------------
  ; agatti.dtsi:1699 - the Adreno register window, on the GX rail with
  ; VDDCX behind it. A probe that powered the CX domain up and then read
  ; this window did not come back.
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $05900000, $0593FFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the graphics processor's register window, which lives behind a power domain the firmware leaves collapsed: it answers only while that domain has been brought up on purpose, and a probe that read it after powering the domain is the third thing to have hung this board"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $05961000, $059617FF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the graphics processor's CX debug block, on the same collapsed power domain as its register window"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  ; The GMU shim window, SPLIT AROUND GPUCC - see the note in the header.
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $0596A000, $0598FFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the graphics power-sequencing window, which sits behind two of the graphics power domains at once"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $05999000, $0599FFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the graphics processor's CX memory window, on the collapsed graphics power domain"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $059A0000, $059AFFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the graphics processor's own address translator, which is behind the graphics CX power domain"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf

  ; ---- EVERYTHING ELSE THE DEVICE TREE PUTS BEHIND A GDSC OR AN
  ;      OFF-BY-DEFAULT CLOCK. DECLARED evidence, refusing rather than
  ;      allowing, which is the safe direction for a weaker source.
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $01613000, $01615FFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "one of the two USB physical-layer blocks, both of which are off until a driver votes for them"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $01B04000, $01B3FFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the crypto engine and its DMA controller, which share a clock nothing has voted for here"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  ; The random-number generator LOOKS always-on and is not: it carries a
  ; managed clock of its own (agatti.dtsi:1004-1008). It is called out
  ; separately because it is exactly the row a reader would assume is safe.
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $04453000, $04453FFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the hardware random-number generator, which despite sitting among always-on blocks has a managed clock of its own that nothing has voted for here"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $04744000, $0474FFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the first storage-card controller, which is behind a managed power domain"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $04784000, $04784FFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the second storage-card controller, which is behind a managed power domain and is not even enabled on this board"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $04E00000, $04E0CCFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the USB host controller, which is behind a power domain that is collapsed until its driver runs"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $04EF8800, $04EF8BFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the USB controller's glue registers, behind the same collapsed power domain as the controller"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $05804000, $05848FFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the IP accelerator block, which is clocked only on a vote from the power manager and is not present in this board's own device tree at all"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $05A00000, $05AEFFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the video codec, which is behind two power domains at once"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $05C11000, $05C79FFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the camera subsystem, which is behind a power domain and is disabled on this board"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $05E00000, $05EB2FFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the display subsystem, which is behind the display power domain"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $05F00000, $05F1FFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the display clock controller, whose own registers need a clock the display power domain supplies"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $06080000, $060800FF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the modem processor's control block"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  ; THE AUDIO SUBSYSTEM IS THE WORST REGION ON THE BOARD. Every window in
  ; it is clocked by a vote held by the audio DSP, so reaching it needs
  ; another processor to be running and voting. Two of its windows are
  ; NOT marked disabled in the device tree, so a reader applying "enabled
  ; means safe" walks straight into them.
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $0A600000, $0A7EFFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the low-power audio subsystem, every window of which is clocked only while the audio processor is running and holding a vote for it - and some of those windows are not marked disabled anywhere, so they look reachable and are not"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $0A950000, $0A95FFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the audio subsystem's second pin-controller window, on the same audio-processor vote as the rest of that block"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $0AB00000, $0AB000FF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the audio processor's control block"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $0C800000, $0CFFFFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the Wi-Fi block, an 8 MB aperture that is dark until its firmware has been loaded and its rails brought up"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  2. SAFE. PROVEN on this board, and the range must be WHOLLY inside.
  ; ==================================================================

  ; THE PIN CONTROLLER. PROVEN: all 127 lines were read and one was
  ; driven and read back, on silicon, from this monitor at this exception
  ; level. It is in the always-on domain and needs nobody's permission.
  ; This is the control that separates "peripheral MMIO is blocked under
  ; this firmware" - which is false - from "that one block is gated",
  ; which is true.
  If QAddrIn(lo, hi, $00500000, $007FFFFF) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_SAFE
  EndIf

  ; THE GRAPHICS CLOCK CONTROLLER. PROVEN: the graphics bring-up probe
  ; reached it bare metal and read its PLL and divider registers, which
  ; were sitting at their reset defaults under this firmware. It is the
  ; one window in the whole graphics region that has answered here - and
  ; it is next door to the register window that hung the board, which is
  ; why the two are named separately rather than lumped together as "the
  ; GPU".
  If QAddrIn(lo, hi, $05990000, $05998FFF) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_SAFE
  EndIf

  ; DRAM. PROVEN in the most direct way there is: this monitor is running
  ; out of it, its transfers land in it, and its payload boot placed an
  ; image in it and hashed it. Two ranges, because there is a
  ; firmware-reserved gap between the first bank and the second - see the
  ; UNKNOWN row for that gap below.
  If QAddrIn(lo, hi, #Q_DRAM_BASE, $7D9FFFFF) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_SAFE
  EndIf
  If QAddrIn(lo, hi, $80000000, #Q_DRAM_TOP - 1) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_SAFE
  EndIf

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  3. UNKNOWN, WITH SOMETHING TO SAY. These are the rows where the
  ;     answer is "nobody has read this here" but the reason a reader
  ;     wants is more specific than that.
  ; ==================================================================

  ; SERIAL ENGINE 4, THE CONSOLE ENGINE, AND THE ONE HONEST DISAGREEMENT
  ; IN THIS FILE. It was reported clocked on the grounds that the
  ; firmware console rides it. That reasoning is exactly the assumption
  ; the first hang disproved: the wrapper is the first thing in the
  ; aperture, it was dark, and the two AHB clocks that gate it gate all
  ; six engines. It may still be true that the boot chain ungates the
  ; engine while it is transmitting. Nobody has read the engine here and
  ; lived to report it, so this says UNKNOWN and says why, rather than
  ; SAFE on an argument or UNCLOCKED on a guess.
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $04A90000, $04A93FFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "QUP serial engine 4, the firmware console's engine: it is reported clocked because the console rides it, but that is the same reasoning that stalled this board once, and no read of it here has ever completed"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNKNOWN
  EndIf

  ; THE ALWAYS-ON SET. The device tree gives these no clock and no power
  ; domain, which on this vendor's trees is how "always on" is spelled.
  ; That is DECLARED evidence and it is almost certainly right - but
  ; almost certainly right is what SAFE must not mean, so they answer
  ; UNKNOWN with the reason stated and are two words away from being
  ; read. This row is why the seam has three answers instead of two.
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $00340000, $0035FFFF) <> 0 Or QAddrHit(lo, hi, $003C0000, $003FFFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "a system-wide configuration block that this SoC's device tree puts in the always-on domain - so it very probably answers, and nobody has read it from here to find out"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNKNOWN
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $01400000, $015EFFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the main clock controller, which the device tree puts in the always-on domain: it is the block you would have to write to in order to ungate anything else, and nobody has read it from here yet"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNKNOWN
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $01B44000, $01B46FFF) <> 0 Or QAddrHit(lo, hi, $04410000, $04411FFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "a fuse or temperature-sensor block the device tree puts in the always-on domain, unread from here"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNKNOWN
  EndIf
  ; Five windows with gaps between them, tested as one span because every
  ; byte of it - window or gap - gets the same answer and only the
  ; sentence would differ, so the sentence says "in or between".
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $01C0A000, $03F9FFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "in or between the five windows of the power-management-chip bus, which the device tree puts in the always-on domain, unread from here"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNKNOWN
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $045F0000, $045F6FFF) <> 0 Or QAddrHit(lo, hi, $04690000, $0469FFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the power manager's shared message memory, which the device tree puts in the always-on domain, unread from here"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNKNOWN
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $0F017000, $0F017FFF) <> 0 Or QAddrHit(lo, hi, $0F111000, $0F128FFF) <> 0 Or QAddrHit(lo, hi, $0F200000, $0F3FFFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "one of the processor-complex blocks - the watchdog, the mailbox, the memory-mapped timer or the interrupt controller - all of which the device tree puts in the always-on domain, and none of which has been read from here"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNKNOWN
  EndIf
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $0C600000, $0C67FFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the system address translator: its global page is in the always-on domain but its per-client register banks are behind four separate votes, so part of this window answers and part of it does not, and which part is which has not been established here"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNKNOWN
  EndIf

  ; THE GAP BETWEEN THE DRAM BANKS. 38 MB with no memory in it, sitting
  ; between two ranges that ARE safe, which makes it exactly the sort of
  ; address a mistyped digit lands on.
  If QAddrHit(lo, hi, $7DA00000, $7FFFFFFF) <> 0
    *gQAddrWhy = "the firmware-reserved gap between this board's first and second memory banks: there is no memory here, and the two ranges either side of it are both fine, which is what makes a mistyped digit land in it"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNKNOWN
  EndIf

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  4. NOTHING KNOWN AT ALL.
  ; ==================================================================
  ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNKNOWN
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwAddrReason()
  ProcedureReturn *gQAddrWhy
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwAddrBlockLo()
  ProcedureReturn gQAddrLo
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwAddrBlockHi()
  ProcedureReturn gQAddrHi
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwAddrReport() - what `info` prints about this board's answer.
;
;  IT PRINTS THE PROVENANCE, not just the verdict. An operator who is
;  about to type `force` needs to know whether they are overriding a
;  measurement or a reading of somebody else's source code, and those two
;  deserve very different amounts of nerve.
;
;  AND IT PRINTS WHERE THE MONITOR IS, for the same reason and with more
;  force on this board than on the other one. Two different questions
;  meet here: whether an access will COMPLETE (the table below) and
;  whether it will DESTROY THE THING THAT IS RUNNING (the monitor
;  window). On the other target the second answer is a compile-time
;  constant and cannot be wrong. Here the firmware decides where this
;  image goes and says so only afterwards, so the answer is read back at
;  run time - which makes this the one board where an operator has a
;  reason to ask, and until now the only way to find out was to aim a
;  payload at it and read the refusal.
;
;  IT ASKS HwMonLo()/HwMonHi(), which is what the overlap test asks. Not
;  the firmware record directly: printing the same fact through a second
;  path is how `info` ends up disagreeing with the guard that decides.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwAddrReport()
  ; WHERE THE MONITOR IS, and where that answer came from.
  Print("  monitor      ")
  PutAddr(HwMonLo())
  Print(" .. ")
  PutAddr(HwMonHi())
  If QEfiImageKnown() = 1
    PrintN("   this monitor's own code and")
    PrintN("               data, from the firmware's LOADED-IMAGE RECORD - read")
    PrintN("               back at run time, because on this board the firmware")
    PrintN("               chooses where the image goes and a compile-time")
    PrintN("               constant would be a guess. This is the pair the")
    PrintN("               overlap test uses, so a payload aimed inside it is")
    PrintN("               refused and the refusal names this window.")
  Else
    PrintN("   WHICH IS THE WHOLE OF MEMORY,")
    PrintN("               and is not where the monitor is. The firmware would")
    PrintN("               not say where it loaded this image, so the board")
    PrintN("               refuses everything rather than claim an extent it")
    PrintN("               cannot prove - which is why boot is refusing every")
    PrintN("               payload. That is the safe direction and it is")
    PrintN("               deliberate; it has not been seen on real firmware.")
  EndIf
  Print("               ")
  PutAddr(#MON_DATA_LO)
  Print(" .. ")
  PutAddr(#MON_DATA_HI)
  PrintN("   a second window the overlap")
  PrintN("               test also refuses. It is a COMPILE-TIME CONSTANT here")
  PrintN("               and nominal - reserved by convention, not measured -")
  PrintN("               so it is named separately rather than folded into the")
  PrintN("               line above, which is not nominal at all.")
  PrintN("  addresses    this board answers a safety question before any command")
  PrintN("               touches an address you typed, because an access to a")
  PrintN("               block whose clock is off does not fail here - it stalls")
  PrintN("               the processor and the board has to be unplugged.")
  PrintN("    SAFE, proven on this board by reading it:")
  PrintN("      00500000..007FFFFF  the pin controller (127 lines read, one driven)")
  PrintN("      05990000..05998FFF  the graphics clock controller (read bare metal)")
  Print("      ")
  PutAddr(#Q_DRAM_BASE)
  PrintN("..7D9FFFFF  memory, first bank")
  Print("      80000000..")
  PutAddr(#Q_DRAM_TOP - 1)
  PrintN("  memory, second and third banks")
  PrintN("    REFUSED, known not to answer:")
  PrintN("      04AC0000..04AC1FFF  the serial block's wrapper - PROVEN: reading")
  PrintN("                          04AC0004 is the command that stalled this board")
  PrintN("      04A80000..04A8FFFF  serial engines 0 to 3, and")
  PrintN("      04A94000..04A97FFF  engine 5 - the wrapper's clocks gate them all")
  PrintN("      05900000..0593FFFF  the graphics register window (behind a")
  PrintN("                          collapsed power domain - the third hang)")
  PrintN("      plus the graphics debug, sequencing, memory and translator")
  PrintN("      windows, the storage, USB, video, camera, display, modem,")
  PrintN("      audio, crypto, random-number and Wi-Fi blocks - each behind a")
  PrintN("      power domain or a clock vote this monitor has never cast.")
  PrintN("    UNKNOWN, and refused for that reason - the notable one:")
  PrintN("      04A90000..04A93FFF  serial engine 4, the console's engine. It is")
  PrintN("                          reported clocked because the console rides it,")
  PrintN("                          which is the reasoning the first hang")
  PrintN("                          disproved, so this board will not claim it.")
  PrintN("    Everything else answers UNKNOWN and is refused, which is")
  PrintN("    ignorance and says so. Type force <address> and repeat the")
  PrintN("    command to go ahead anyway; help force explains why it is two")
  PrintN("    lines and not a flag.")
EndProcedure
